    "https://employment.bryant.edu/postings/4835": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Global Supply Chain Management",
      "description": "Bryant University Employment Opportunities | Assistant Professor, Global Supply Chain Management Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Academics at Bryant University Leadership Rankings and Accreditations Living in Rhode Island Contact Us Assistant Professor, Global Supply Chain Management Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Job Title Assistant Professor, Global Supply Chain Management Full Time / Part Time Full Time Job Type Faculty Reports To Chair, Department of Management Position Summary Bryant University’s College of Business invites applications for an Assistant Professor of Supply Chain Management. The candidate must demonstrate excellence in teaching, research, and service. They must demonstrate excellence in teaching courses in operations and supply chain management including application of AI in the classroom, and the potential for consistent scholarly publications in peer-reviewed journals. Bryant is recognized as a top 1 percent national leader in student education outcomes and ROI and regularly receives high rankings from U.S. News and World Report, QS Stars, Money, Bloomberg Businessweek, Wall Street Journal, and College Factual. Bryant’s strategic plan, Vision 2030 , redefines higher education through a commitment to providing students with a transformational learning experience. Vision 2030 accelerates advancements in academic innovation, experiential learning, vibrant student life, Division I athletics, and a connected community that fosters belonging and success. Principal Accountabilities The successful candidate will be expected to teach in our undergraduate ( BSBA and BSIB ) as well as graduate ( MBA ) programs related to Operations and Global Supply Chain Management. The area of specialization/certification that will be given preference for this position is Supply Chain Management ( SCM ). Applicants will be expected to have expertise in a SCM -related function (e.g. logistics, operations management, planning, sourcing, forecasting, and strategy) and demonstrate a proven ability to engage in an environment that emphasizes cross-functional interaction and integration, both within and outside traditional supply chain management areas. Areas where expertise is desired include (but are not limited to) healthcare operations and management, supply chain strategy, analytics and AI, operations management, global sourcing, and international trade and logistics. The successful candidate will also teach MGT 201: Introduction to Operations Management, a required course for undergraduate business majors that focuses on supply chain content. The position is ideal for recent PhD graduates seeking strong teaching experience in an applied, student-centered environment. The successful candidates will also demonstrate the potential, as their career progresses, to conduct a variety of scholarly activities that support research and teaching, e.g., establishing connections with the business community, developing new courses, building new programs, and writing research grants. This is along with service activities that include participating in and leading new initiatives, mentoring students, and being an active participant in departmental and institutional activities. Qualifications Candidates must have a Ph.D. or D.B.A. in Operations Management, Supply Chain Management, or related area, from an AACSB -International accredited school. ABD with completion expected by August 1, 2027, from an AACSB -International accredited school will be considered. Candidates should be committed to teaching excellence, establishing a scholarly publication record consistent with AACSB standards, and serving as active members of the Bryant community. The demonstrated capacity to conduct research that speaks to both academic and managerial audiences is highly desirable. The successful candidate must be able to teach courses at both the undergraduate ( BSBA and BSIB ) and graduate ( MBA ) level. In addition: Demonstrated potential for excellence in teaching undergraduate operations management, supply chain management, or related quantitative/analytical courses. Ability to integrate AI in Supply Chain Management courses. Strong communication, presentation, and interpersonal skills. Ability to work effectively with diverse student populations and contribute to an inclusive learning environment. Prior teaching experience, especially in Operations Management, Supply Chain Management, or analytics courses. Demonstrated ability to use active learning and experiential activities. Potential for ongoing, long-term contribution to Bryant University’s teaching mission. Industry experience and a willingness to cultivate and engage industry with teaching and research activities. A willingness to provide student focused service to the Management Department and the College of Business. An ability to publish in peer-reviewed academic journals. Working Environment At Bryant University, we believe that our strength lies in the richness of our campus community and the unique perspectives that shape it. By connecting academic and social experiences, we create an environment where individuals feel valued, respected, and supported. Our commitment to fostering belonging and well-being ensures that everyone has the opportunity to thrive and contribute meaningfully. We encourage applications from those who appreciate the wide range of experiences and backgrounds that make our community exceptional. Discover more about how we cultivate a welcoming and collaborative campus culture that drives excellence. Physical Capacities Posting Detail Information Posting Number FAC01641 Open Date 08/21/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Applicant Special Instructions Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter/Letter of Application Optional Documents Teaching Philosophy Other Document Letter(s) of Recommendation Teaching Evaluation(s) Research Statement Office of Human Resources 1150 Douglas Pike Smithfield, RI 02918 Smithfield, RI Privacy Policy Web Accessibility Emergency Information © The Trustees of Bryant University bryant.edu Bryant Campus Map Campus Events To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
